      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14165760/assets/e86f0e2fab2e18a2c5c8991292ed9c2bb36d953758b0e5e217d952d9524325ba.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of the hypotenuse of the ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle above?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's refer to our standard $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14165760/assets/77da0f6ea00a2d1877198387967157f62011356e859ebd2c2cd7cabea074fa85.png)\n\nIn any ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is twice the length of the shorter leg.\n\nThe leg opposite the ${30}^{∘}$ angle is the shorter leg of the triangle. So here, the shorter leg has length $x=6.$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse must be\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 6=12.\n$$",
